# Kuma push credentials for this role's heartbeat scripts — managed by Ansible (k3s role).
#
# Kept out of the scripts themselves, which land 0755 and are therefore readable by every local
# account. `no_log: true` on their template tasks hides the value from Ansible's OUTPUT, not from
# the filesystem — until 2026-08-23 four of these scripts carried their token inline anyway, so
# any local user could read one and forge or suppress a heartbeat, hiding a real outage.
#
# Same split, and the same reasoning, as live-drift-check.env and ping.env: 0640 root:{{ sys_user }}
# so both the root crons (etcd-snapshot, manifest-prune) and the {{ sys_user }} crons (backup
# health, disk health) can read it, and nothing else can.
#
# Sourced by the scripts directly rather than exported by the cron line — unlike live-drift-check,
# whose Python consumer needs `set -a`. A missing or unreadable file leaves these empty, and each
# script already treats an empty token as "skip the push and say so" rather than failing the run.
# Single-quoted because this file is `.`-sourced by root and {{ sys_user }} shell scripts, which
# means an unquoted value is shell input rather than data: whitespace would truncate the token,
# and `$(...)` or a backtick would execute as whichever user the cron runs as. Kuma tokens are
# alphanumeric today, so this is defence in depth against a future rotation producing something
# less tame — not a live defect (2026-08-23b review L5).
LONGHORN_BACKUP_PUSH_TOKEN='{{ longhorn_backup_push_token | default("") }}'
DANIEL_BOX_DISK_PUSH_TOKEN='{{ daniel_box_disk_push_token | default("") }}'
ETCD_SNAPSHOT_PUSH_TOKEN='{{ etcd_snapshot_push_token | default("") }}'
MANIFEST_PRUNE_PUSH_TOKEN='{{ manifest_prune_push_token | default("") }}'
REMEMBER_LOGS_PUSH_TOKEN='{{ remember_logs_push_token | default("") }}'
